Named a Best Book of 2024 by Smithsonian * Kirkus * Chicago Review of Books * Emancipator * Ms. Magazine Black resistance to white supremacy is often reduced to a simple binary, between Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.’s nonviolence and Malcolm X’s “by any means necessary.” In We Refuse, historian Kellie Carter Jackson urges us to move past this false choice, offering an unflinching examination of the breadth of Black responses to white oppression, particularly those pioneered by Black women. The dismissal of “Black violence” as an illegitimate form of resistance is itself a manifestation of white supremacy, a distraction from the insidious, unrelenting violence of structural racism.","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.082,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.196,"traits":[{"id":"narrative","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"resistance\""]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":false,"publicFame":18,"criticalRenown":22,"controversy":12,"llmAffinity":86,"affinityResidual":14.32,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.18,"criticalRenown":0.18,"controversy":0.15,"llmAffinity":0.84},"tags":{"craft":"prose-style","evidence":"archival-research","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"displaySummary":"The dismissal of “Black violence” as an illegitimate form of resistance is itself a manifestation of white supremacy, a distraction from the insidious, unrelenting violence of structural racism.","primarySubject":"History","primaryTopic":"Civil Rights & Racial Justice","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"History","score":3,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubj